package Libraries.Multitasking

/*
    Key's Python plugin uses the scheduler, which is in the Python plugin of
    Libraries.Multitasking.Task. This use statement makes a Python program
    that uses a Key, such as one with an IntegerInSync, include Task's
    plugin too.
*/
use Libraries.Multitasking.Task

/*
The Key class lets one line at a time into a piece of code, like a single
restroom key behind a counter. Take waits until no other line has the key,
then takes it. Return gives it back, so the next waiting line can take it.

In-sync containers, such as HashTableInSync, use a Key inside, so the people
using them don't have to. A Key is for code that needs several steps to
happen as one: no other line holding the same key can run in between.

Put the code between Take and Return in a check, and return the key in the
detect block too, so the key comes back even if the code has an error.
(Not in an always block: today the compiler crashes on a check that has an
always block but no detect block.)

The same line may take the key more than once, for example when one action
holding the key calls another. Each Take needs its own Return. Everything a
line did before Return is seen by the next line that takes the key.

Attribute: Example

use Libraries.Multitasking.Key
class Counter
    Key key
    integer count = 0

    action Increase
        key:Take()
        check
            count = count + 1
        detect problem
            key:Return()
            alert(problem)
        end
        key:Return()
    end
end
*/
class Key
    /*
        This action waits until no other line has the key, then takes it.
        If this line already has the key, it takes it again right away; it
        then needs one more Return.

        Attribute: Example
        use Libraries.Multitasking.Key

        Key key
        key:Take()
        // only one line at a time gets here
        key:Return()
    */
    system action Take

    /*
        This action gives the key back, so the next waiting line can take
        it. Only the line that has the key may return it.

        Attribute: Example
        use Libraries.Multitasking.Key

        Key key
        key:Take()
        // only one line at a time gets here
        key:Return()
    */
    system action Return

    /*
        This action gives the key back, waits until another line has taken
        it and returned it, then takes it again. Use it when a line holding
        the key finds it can't go on yet, for example a queue that is
        empty: it lets other lines in to change things, and comes back to
        look again. Always check again after it returns, in a loop, since
        the other line may have changed something else.

        The line must have the key. If it took the key more than once, it
        gives all of it back, and takes it back as many times.

        Attribute: Example
        use Libraries.Multitasking.Key

        Key key
        key:Take()
        repeat while not IsReady()
            key:ReturnAndWait()
        end
        // the key is held again here
        key:Return()
    */
    system action ReturnAndWait

    /*
        This action gives the key back and waits, as ReturnAndWait does, but
        gives up after a number of milliseconds. Either way, the line has
        the key again when it returns.

        Attribute: Parameter milliseconds How long to wait at most.

        Attribute: Returns True if another line used the key, false if the time ran out.

        Attribute: Example
        use Libraries.Multitasking.Key

        Key key
        key:Take()
        boolean used = key:ReturnAndWait(1000)
        key:Return()
    */
    system action ReturnAndWait(number milliseconds) returns boolean
end
